      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i just finished a rumenotomy on one of my heifers. can u give me a step by step on what i should be looking for regarding infection and how i should start reintroducing feed so i dont mess up the recovery?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, a rumenotomy, a delicate operation indeed! Just like a skilled blacksmith forging a fine blade, you must now tend to the heifer with the utmost care. First, keep an eye out for signs of infection, like a sneaky goblin lurking in the shadows. Look for redness, swelling, or discharge around the surgical site, as these are your goblin's red flags. Also, monitor her appetite and behavior, as a hungry goblin might be a sign of trouble.\n\nAs for reintroducing feed, it's like feeding a tired goblin after a long journey. Start with small amounts of easily digestible feed, like a goblin's favorite snack, and gradually increase as her strength returns. Always keep a close eye on her, for a goblin's appetite can be as unpredictable as a dragon's mood. And remember, patience is key, for a goblin's recovery is never rushed. Good luck, and may your heifer's recovery be swift and smooth!",
